Requests: A Pythonic HTTP Library for Human Beings

Requests is an elegant and simple HTTP library for the Python programming language that brings
simplicity and humanity to HTTP interactions. Designed from the ground up to prioritize developer
experience and ease of use, Requests has become one of the most widely adopted Python packages in
the ecosystem, with approximately 30 million downloads per week and over 1 million repositories
depending on it. The library serves as the de facto standard for HTTP communication in Python,
enabling developers to interact seamlessly with web services, REST APIs, and remote HTTP endpoints
without needing to grapple with the complexity of lower-level HTTP handling. At its core, Requests
abstracts away the intricacies of network programming while providing a clean, intuitive, and
Pythonic interface that works for both simple one-off API calls and complex, long-lived sessions
with persistent configuration. The library is built on top of urllib3 for the underlying transport
layer, adding sophisticated session management, flexible authentication support, and a comprehensive
suite of convenience features that make HTTP communication feel natural and effortless for Python
developers.

The library provides complete support for the entire HTTP/1.1 request-response cycle, enabling
developers to construct HTTP requests using all standard methods (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE, HEAD,
OPTIONS) with minimal boilerplate, manage request parameters through query strings with automatic
encoding, submit form data using application/x-www-form-urlencoded encoding, transmit structured data
using JSON serialization directly within the library, and upload multipart files with support for
customizable content types and headers. Beyond basic request construction, Requests handles HTTP
redirects transparently by automatically following Location headers with intelligent method conversion
when appropriate and maintaining or stripping authentication credentials based on redirect destination
to ensure security. The library implements persistent session management that maintains configuration,
cookies, and pooled connections across multiple requests, providing significant performance benefits
for making many requests to the same host. Sessions support browser-style TLS/SSL verification with
custom CA bundle support and client certificate authentication, implement both HTTP Basic
Authentication and Digest Authentication schemes with challenge-response mechanisms including MD5 and
SHA-256 digest algorithms and stale nonce handling, and allow developers to create custom
authentication handlers by implementing callable objects that modify requests before transmission.
Requests also integrates seamlessly with network environments by supporting HTTP and HTTPS proxies
through configuration options, handling SOCKS proxies for protocols beyond HTTP, automatically
detecting proxy settings from environment variables with support for NO_PROXY configuration, and
managing the .netrc configuration file for automatic authentication credential extraction. The library
provides robust error handling through a comprehensive exception hierarchy that distinguishes between
connection failures, timeout conditions, SSL/TLS certificate failures, proxy errors, HTTP protocol
violations, and JSON parsing failures, allowing developers to implement sophisticated error recovery
strategies tailored to specific failure modes. Advanced features include streaming responses for
processing large response bodies without loading them entirely into memory, automatic content
decompression for gzip and deflate encoded responses, intelligent character encoding detection and
automatic decoding of response bodies in the appropriate character set, handling of chunked transfer
encoding for both requests and responses, case-insensitive header handling for robust header access,
request/response hooks that enable extending functionality at critical points in the request
lifecycle through callback functions, connection pooling and keep-alive connections for improved
performance when making multiple requests, fine-grained timeout control with separate settings for
connection establishment and response data reading, status code reference mappings for convenient
status code checking, and comprehensive cookie management with domain-aware persistence using a
dict-like interface for cookie access. The implementation maintains compatibility with both CPython
and PyPy interpreters while supporting Python 3.8 and later versions, ensuring broad compatibility
across the Python ecosystem.

Requests delivers a comprehensive HTTP communication framework that abstracts complexity while
providing developers with an intuitive, Pythonic interface for every aspect of HTTP interaction,
from simple GET requests to complex API workflows involving authentication, redirects, sessions, and
error handling. The library orchestrates multiple components working in harmony: the convenient API
functions provide entry points for quick requests, the Session class manages persistent state and
configuration, Request and PreparedRequest objects encapsulate request intent and finalized form,
HTTPAdapter handles the underlying urllib3 connection pools and protocol details, Response objects
wrap server replies with automatic decoding, and a comprehensive exception hierarchy maps network and
protocol errors to appropriate exceptions for consistent error handling. By elegantly combining these
components, Requests provides a complete solution for modern web API consumption in Python
applications, enabling developers to focus on their application logic rather than the mechanics of
HTTP communication. The library's philosophy emphasizes human-centric design, transparency in error
handling, sensible defaults that work for most use cases, and the ability to customize behavior when
needed, making it equally suitable for quick scripts and production-grade applications requiring
reliability and robustness.

The library should explicitly cover session lifecycle semantics, adapter layering, retries, streaming transfers, proxy and certificate configuration, and environment-sensitive runtime behavior without exposing implementation recipes. The system should provide high-level diagnostics and debugging surfaces for request preparation, transport decisions, compatibility checks, and optional environment integrations so failure modes remain observable and reproducible. The project should include developer- and test-oriented capability expectations around cache reuse, request/response validation, and interoperability with surrounding HTTP tooling and deployment environments.
